# BitCorn Lightning — per-install operator secrets
#
# Create `.env` on the Umbrel host at exactly this path and fill in values:
#
#   /home/umbrel/umbrel/app-data/bitcorn-lightning-node/.env
#
# That is ${UMBREL_ROOT}/app-data/bitcorn-lightning-node/.env — the app-data
# directory. NOT the app-store clone, and NOT a git checkout.
#
# ⚠ A `.env` anywhere else — including next to this file in a repo checkout —
# is NEVER READ, AND NOTHING REPORTS IT. exports.sh tests for the app-data
# path and skips loading when it is absent, so the app still starts, at exit
# 0, with every operator value blank. The first symptom is a feature behaving
# as if unconfigured (POST /api/valuation/manual returning 503
# valuation_worker_not_configured), not an error at startup.
#
# Docker Compose does NOT auto-read this file. Umbrel's legacy-compat
# `source_app` runs exports.sh before invoking compose; exports.sh sources the
# app-data `.env` under `set -a` so compose's `${VAR:-}` interpolation picks
# the values up. Compose's `env_file:` directive was tried in v1.11.2 and
# reverted in v1.11.3 — under umbreld's multi-`--file` invocation it resolves
# relative paths against umbreld's fragment directory, not the app's.
#
# `.env` is gitignored repo-wide — the actual secret never touches git. The
# app-data copy survives app updates; it is wiped only on uninstall.
#
# After editing .env, restart the app:
#   sudo umbreld client apps.restart.mutate --appId bitcorn-lightning-node

# ── Valuation manual-input submission (treasury node → Cloudflare Worker) ──
# Shared HMAC secret that signs POST /valuation/manual payloads. The same
# value must be set on the Worker via:
#   cd cloudflare-worker && npx wrangler secret put VALUATION_SUBMIT_HMAC
# Generate a fresh 32-byte hex secret with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# If left unset, POST /api/valuation/manual returns 503 valuation_worker_not_configured
# and the treasury web UI's "Save All" button reports a 503 toast.
VALUATION_SUBMIT_HMAC=

# Optional — URL of the Worker that exposes POST /valuation/manual.
# Defaults to the value of COINBASE_WORKER_URL already set in docker-compose.yml.
# Override only if you split the Onramp and Valuation Workers.
# VALUATION_WORKER_URL=https://bitcorn-onramp.example.workers.dev

# ── Cloudflare Tunnel (Phase 4 — named tunnel for cross-tailnet members) ──
# A persistent named Cloudflare Tunnel that exposes the API container at a
# stable hostname (e.g., https://treasury.<your-domain>). Required so members
# on different tailnets than the treasury can reach it; tailnet-private IPs
# do not work for cross-tailnet members (Stage 4.5 reachability gap).
#
# To activate Phase 4:
#   1. Create a tunnel in Cloudflare dashboard (Zero Trust → Networks →
#      Tunnels → Create a tunnel → Cloudflared → name "bitcorn-treasury").
#   2. Copy the TUNNEL_TOKEN from the tunnel install screen (long base64).
#      Paste below — NO surrounding quotes, NO trailing whitespace.
#   3. In the Cloudflare dashboard's "Public Hostname" tab for this tunnel,
#      route treasury.<your-domain> → http://bitcorn-lightning-node_api_1:3101
#      and let the dashboard create the CNAME record automatically.
#   4. Set COMPOSE_PROFILES=tunnel below to activate the cloudflared sidecar.
#   5. Update the Worker secret to point at the new URL:
#        cd cloudflare-worker
#        npx wrangler secret put TREASURY_API_URL    # paste https://treasury.<your-domain>
#        npx wrangler deploy
#
# Operators NOT on Phase 4 (no Cloudflare account, staying on Phase 1
# ephemeral, or tailnet-only deployment) leave both values unset and
# the cloudflared sidecar stays off.
#
# Full setup procedure: docs/operator-runbook.md (Cloudflare Tunnel section).
TUNNEL_TOKEN=

# Activates the cloudflared sidecar in docker-compose.yml. Set to `tunnel`
# only after TUNNEL_TOKEN is populated above. Leave unset for non-tunnel
# deployments — the cloudflared service will stay off.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=

# ── BASE rail (stablecoin) — chain selection + SIWE verification RPC ──
# All three are OPTIONAL. ⚠ THEY NO LONGER ALL DEFAULT TO BASE SEPOLIA, and the
# previous guidance here ("leave unset unless cutting over; the deploy-day flip
# is pure config") was wrong in a way worth naming: it assumed one operator
# editing one file. Members administer their own nodes, so a value that is only
# correct after a per-node edit is a value that is wrong on every member node.
# Anything that must be right everywhere now ships as the default in
# docker-compose.yml; only what CANNOT ship stays here.

# Numeric chainId for SIWE wallet binding (API) — which chain member wallet
# signatures are pinned to.
#
# DEFAULT IS NOW 8453 (Base mainnet), set in docker-compose.yml. You do not need
# to set this for production. It must agree with the web bundle's build-time
# VITE_BASE_CHAIN_ID or every wallet registration fails with chain_id_mismatch.
#
# SET IT ONLY FOR LOCAL/TESTNET WORK: a locally built web image defaults to
# 84532 (app/web/Dockerfile), so a local stack needs 84532 here to match. The
# mismatch fails closed and names itself, so you will know.
# BASE_CHAIN_ID=84532

# RPC endpoint the API uses to verify ERC-1271 smart-wallet signatures
# (Coinbase Smart Wallet) during SIWE wallet binding. Must point at the
# SAME chain as BASE_CHAIN_ID.
#
# ⚠ THIS ONE CANNOT SHIP A DEFAULT — the URL embeds an API key, so there is no
# value that is safe to commit. Its absence degrades PARTIALLY rather than
# failing: EOA wallets (MetaMask, WalletConnect) still verify locally, while
# smart-contract wallets are refused with 503
# smart_wallet_verification_unavailable. Coinbase Smart Wallet is the DEFAULT
# connector in the web app, so leaving this unset turns the most common wallet
# away on an otherwise healthy node.
# BASE_RPC_URL=

# Blocks below the BASE tip before a Settled event counts as committed
# (reorg safety). Default: 64 (~2 min at 2s blocks). Lower only for dev.
# BASE_CONFIRMATION_DEPTH=64
